[C2] OPTION B — bond user_ref_clk to its own pad (SDC HALF ONLY)

READ THIS FIRST. THIS PATCH IS NOT ALL OF OPTION B.
    Option B is a HARDWARE change. This file is the constraint half. Applying it
    WITHOUT the five changes listed under "WHAT THIS PATCH CANNOT DO" leaves the
    design with a create_clock on a port that does not exist, which will not
    elaborate. Land them together or not at all.

WHAT THIS DOES
    Declares user_ref_clk as a primary clock on a new USER_REF_CLK pad, gives it
    uncertainty and drive characterisation, and puts it at the head of the D2D
    transmit group — so the existing `-asynchronous` group becomes true as
    written instead of cutting a boundary the tool can see is synchronous.

    Clocks         33 -> 34        (+1 create_clock on USER_REF_CLK)
    Clock groups    5 -> 5         (3 / 10 / 17 / 3 / 1; 34 clocks, all grouped)
    set_false_path  7 -> 7         (unchanged)
    set_max_delay   0 -> 0         (unchanged)
    set_clock_uncertainty 68 -> 70 (+2 for the new clock)
    set_input_transition  21 -> 23 (+2 for the new port)

CORRECTING THE [C2] BLOCK: OPTION B IS NOT "NO SDC EDIT AT ALL"
    The block being replaced claims Option B needs no constraint change. That is
    wrong, and it is the kind of wrong that ships. Every transmit clock is a
    create_generated_clock whose -source traces back through an INTERNAL pin
    ($WL/user_hsclk, $WL/pad_clk_tx) to whatever primary clock feeds the Wlink.
    Remove the pad alias without adding a primary clock here and that trace ends
    at an unclocked port: D2D_TX_CLK_0 and D2D_TX_WORD_CLK_0..7 lose their
    master, ~1.5k transmit-word flops go back to having no waveform, and the run
    still reports success.

APPLIES TO
    ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/constraints.sdc
    sha256 90b776a92cdd67ffe19db5d8ca8572ddb6cb0c9b179a57a9d8287eba37cc0a80
    (edited by concurrent sessions; re-read the [C2] block if the hash has moved)

    tidelink_constraints.sdc is UNCHANGED, and that is the payoff of a decision
    already taken: its TX word clocks are sourced at $WL/user_hsclk rather than
    [get_ports CLK] precisely so they survive this option. Had they been anchored
    at the CLK port they would now be sourced on a clock that no longer reaches
    them — and it would still elaborate and still report clean.

WHAT THIS PATCH CANNOT DO — the hardware change request
    1. ASIC/tech_wrappers/tsmc65/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads.v
       Remove the alias at :137-139; add a PDDW04DGZ_G input pad instance
       (uPAD_USER_REF_CLK_I, the same cell and tie pattern as uPAD_CLK_I at
       :266-273) driving soc_user_ref_clk; add the USER_REF_CLK port.
    2. sys_desc/chip_boundary/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.yaml
       Delete `- { soc_port: user_ref_clk, const: "sys_fclk" }` from the ALIASED
       CLOCKS block (:178-199) and add user_ref_clk as a bonded chip_port.
       rtc_clk STAYS aliased. Regenerate the wrapper; `make chip-boundary` then
       reports 24 pad cells / bonded 36 instead of 23 / 35.
    3. ASIC/genus-innovus/scripts/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads.io
       Add the pad to the top side beside uPAD_CLK_I. The side is relative-
       spaced, so the 17 existing top pads at space=55 become 18 at space=50
       (18 x 25 + 17 x 50 + 30 = 1330, and 50 = 20+20+10 is filler-legal).
    4. ASIC/genus-innovus/scripts/place_bondpads.tcl
       The eight name lists hand-encode the outer/inner bond-pad parity of the
       .io ring order. APPEND at the end of the top side rather than inserting
       mid-side, or every pad after the insertion point reparents. Nothing in
       the tree enforces this and `make chip-boundary` will not catch it.
    5. THE BOARD. A clock source for the new pin. A DISTINCT source: if the
       board feeds it from a buffered copy of the oscillator that drives CLK,
       the two clocks share a source again and `-asynchronous` is a fiction one
       buffer further out. Say so on the schematic.

PAD BUDGET — THE ANSWER IS THERE IS ROOM, AND IT IS NOT CLOSE
    The [C2] block prices this option at "one more bonded clock pad out of the
    ring's budget". Measured, that half of the cost is ~zero:
      * 82 pad cells (48 signal + 34 power/ground) + 4 corners + 325 fillers.
      * Every signal pad is 25.000 um wide (patched vendor LEF).
      * 4,070 um of IO filler is inserted across the four sides, and the
        SMALLEST single contiguous gap anywhere in the ring is 42 um — 17 um
        more than a whole pad cell. On three sides the gaps are 55 um.
      * The v1 "pad budget" is a SCOPE DECISION recorded in commit e809fbf
        ("back to the intended 23-pad budget", 46 -> 23), not a geometric limit.
        Nothing in pads.v, the .io file, floorplan.tcl or place_bondpads.tcl
        claims the ring is physically full.
    What does NOT exist is a spare already-instantiated pad to repurpose: all 48
    signal pads are bound. SE and TEST are the only candidates and the boundary
    spec reserves them deliberately (yaml:131-132) for a future muxed scan
    chain. Adding a pad is cheaper than taking either.

